// A bounded search, and how a fault is reported.

// The first index in [i, hi) holding byte c, or hi.
//
// Bounded rather than open-ended because the two callers are both asking about
// one text node: an '&' or a ';' belonging to a later part of the document is
// not an answer, and an unbounded search that then rejects its answer has
// already walked the rest of the file to find it.
xml_scan(int i, int hi, int c) {
  while(i < hi && xml_ch(i) != c) {
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int i;

// Stop on malformed input.
//
// The message and the byte offset are kept, and the read position is driven to
// the end of the input so that the caller's `while (xml_next() != 0)` ends on
// its own -- `id` has no break and no exception. Only the first fault is
// recorded: everything after one is a guess about a document we have already
// lost track of.
xml_fail(string msg, int i) {
  if(xml_err() == "") {
    sset((import xml_strs), 1, msg);
    lset((import xml_st), 5, i);
  }
  lset((import xml_st), 0, xml_len());
} return int 5;

// Step over the byte at i and answer kind k. The counterpart of xml_need's
// success branch, and separate from it because the branch is a whole block.
xml_pass(int i, int k) {
  lset((import xml_st), 0, i + 1);
} return int k;
